{title2=Analysis II}

= i
{parent=3e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A sequence of functions $f_n:A\to\mathbb R$ <uniform convergence>[converges uniformly] to $f$ when, for every $\varepsilon>0$, there is an $N$ such that
$$
n\geq N\quad\Longrightarrow\quad |f_n(x)-f(x)|<\varepsilon
$$
for every $x\in A$. Equivalently, $\lVert f_n-f\rVert_\infty\to0$ in the <supremum norm>.

**Yes.** Fix $x_0\in A$ and $\varepsilon>0$. Uniform convergence gives $N$ such that $|f_N(x)-f(x)|<\varepsilon/3$ for every $x\in A$. Since the <continuous function> $f_N$ is continuous at $x_0$, there is a $\delta>0$ such that $|x-x_0|<\delta$ implies $|f_N(x)-f_N(x_0)|<\varepsilon/3$. The <triangle inequality> then gives
$$
|f(x)-f(x_0)|
\leq |f(x)-f_N(x)|+|f_N(x)-f_N(x_0)|+|f_N(x_0)-f(x_0)|
<\varepsilon.
$$
This proves the <uniform limit theorem>: $f$ is continuous.

= ii
{parent=3e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**No.** For each $x>0$, $e^{-nx}\to0$, whereas $e^{-n0}=1$ for every $n$. The <pointwise convergence>[pointwise limit] on $[0,1]$ is therefore
$$
f(x)=\begin{cases}1,&x=0,\\0,&x>0.\end{cases}
$$
This limit is not <continuous function>[continuous] at $0$. Since every $f_n(x)=e^{-nx}$ is continuous, the <uniform limit theorem> rules out uniform convergence. Directly, $|f_n(x)-f(x)|=e^{-nx}$ for $x>0$, whose <supremum> over $(0,1]$ is $1$ for every $n$.

= iii
{parent=3e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**No.** Define
$$
f_n(x)=\sqrt{x^2+\frac1n}.
$$
Each $f_n$ is a <differentiable function> on $[-1,1]$, while
$$
0\leq f_n(x)-|x|
=\frac{1/n}{\sqrt{x^2+1/n}+|x|}
\leq\frac1{\sqrt n}.
$$
**Thus $f_n$ converges uniformly to the <absolute value function> $|x|$, which is not differentiable at $0$. Uniform convergence alone therefore does not preserve differentiability.**

= 4F
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Complex Analysis}

= Solution
{parent=4F}

The <Cauchy integral formula> says that if $f$ is <holomorphic function>[holomorphic] on a neighbourhood of the closed disc $\overline{D(z_0;R)}$, then for $z\in D(z_0;R)$,
$$
f(z)=\frac1{2\pi i}\int_{|\zeta-z_0|=R}\frac{f(\zeta)}{\zeta-z}\,d\zeta.
$$
In particular, at the centre,
$$
f(z_0)=\frac1{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}f(z_0+re^{i\theta})\,d\theta
$$
for every $0<r<R$.

If $f(z_0)=0$, the assumed bound gives $|f(z)|\leq0$, so $f=0$. Otherwise define $g=f/f(z_0)$. Then $g(z_0)=1$ and $|g|\leq1$. Applying the centre formula and taking <real part>[real parts] gives
$$
1=\frac1{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}\operatorname{Re}g(z_0+re^{i\theta})\,d\theta,
\qquad \operatorname{Re}g\leq|g|\leq1.
$$
The continuous nonnegative function $1-\operatorname{Re}g$ has integral zero, so it vanishes around the circle. Equality $\operatorname{Re}g=1$ together with $|g|\leq1$ forces $g=1$. This holds for every $r<R$, hence $f(z)=f(z_0)$ throughout the disc. This is the equality case underlying the <maximum modulus principle>.

= 5D
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Methods}

= Solution
{parent=5D}

Introduce the <Cauchy approximate identity>
$$
\delta_\varepsilon(x)=\frac{\varepsilon}{\pi(\varepsilon^2+x^2)}.
$$
It is an <approximate identity> converging to the <Dirac delta function> $\delta$, and $g_\varepsilon=\delta_\varepsilon'$. For a smooth rapidly decaying <test function> $\phi$, <integration by parts> gives
$$
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\phi(x)g_\varepsilon(x)\,dx
=-\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\phi'(x)\delta_\varepsilon(x)\,dx
\longrightarrow-\phi'(0).
$$
By the definition of the <derivative of the Dirac delta>, $\langle\delta',\phi\rangle=-\phi'(0)$. Therefore the limit as a <distribution> is
$$
\boxed{g_\varepsilon\longrightarrow\delta'}.
$$

= 6B
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Quantum Mechanics}

= a
{parent=6b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a <wavefunction> $\Psi(x,t)$, the <probability density> and <probability current> are
$$
\rho=|\Psi|^2,
\qquad
j=\frac{\hbar}{2mi}\left(\Psi^*\frac{\partial\Psi}{\partial x}-\Psi\frac{\partial\Psi^*}{\partial x}\right).
$$
Multiply the <Schrodinger equation> by $\Psi^*$, multiply its <complex conjugate> by $\Psi$, and subtract. The real <potential energy> terms cancel, leaving
$$
\frac{\partial|\Psi|^2}{\partial t}
=-\frac{\partial}{\partial x}
\left[\frac{\hbar}{2mi}
\left(\Psi^*\Psi_x-\Psi\Psi_x^*\right)\right],
$$
which is the <probability continuity equation> $\rho_t+j_x=0$.

For a <stationary state>, $\rho_t=0$, so $j_x=0$ and $j$ is constant in space. A <normalizable wavefunction> has vanishing current at spatial infinity, forcing this constant to be zero. By contrast the nonnormalizable <plane wave>
$$
\Psi(x,t)=A e^{i(kx-\omega t)}
$$
has stationary density $|A|^2$ and nonzero current
$$
\boxed{j=\frac{\hbar k}{m}|A|^2}
$$
when $k\ne0$.

= b
{parent=6b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <momentum operator> in the position representation is $\widehat p=-i\hbar\,d/dx$. Differentiating the given <Gaussian wave packet> gives
$$
\frac{d\Psi}{dx}=(-2\alpha x+ik)\Psi,
\qquad
\Psi^*\widehat p\Psi=(2i\hbar\alpha x+\hbar k)|\Psi|^2.
$$
The probability density $|\Psi|^2$ is an <even function>, so the term proportional to the <odd function> $x$ has integral zero. Normalization supplies $\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}|\Psi|^2dx=1$, and hence
$$
\boxed{\langle p\rangle=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\Psi^*\widehat p\Psi\,dx=\hbar k}.
$$

= 7A
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Electromagnetism}

= Solution
{parent=7A}

In vacuum the <Maxwell equations> are
$$
\nabla\mathbin{\cdot}E=0,
\qquad \nabla\mathbin{\cdot}B=0,
\qquad \nabla\times E=-\frac{\partial B}{\partial t},
\qquad \nabla\times B=\frac1{c^2}\frac{\partial E}{\partial t}.
$$
Substitution of the complex <plane electromagnetic wave> amplitudes into these equations gives
$$
k\mathbin{\cdot}E_0=0,
\qquad
B_0=\frac{k\times E_0}{\omega},
\qquad
\omega^2=c^2|k|^2.
$$
Thus the electric field must be <transverse polarization>[transverse] to $k$; taking the positive-frequency branch gives $\omega=c|k|$.

For $E_0=E(1,i,0)$, $k=k\widehat z$, and $\varphi=kz-\omega t$, the real fields are
$$
E(x,t)=E(\cos\varphi,-\sin\varphi,0),
\qquad
B(x,t)=\frac Ec(\sin\varphi,\cos\varphi,0).
$$
The electric vector rotates with constant magnitude, so this is <circular polarization>. Its <Poynting vector> is
$$
\boxed{S=\frac1{\mu_0}E\times B
=\frac{E^2}{\mu_0c}\widehat z}.
$$
The Poynting vector is the electromagnetic <energy flux>: its direction is the direction of propagation and its magnitude is the power crossing unit area.

= 8C
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Numerical Analysis}

= Solution
{parent=8C}

<Gaussian elimination> without row exchanges gives the <LU decomposition>
$$
A=LU,
\qquad
L=\begin{pmatrix}
1&0&0&0\\
2&1&0&0\\
1&1&1&0\\
-2&-1&-1&1
\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
U=\begin{pmatrix}
3&2&-3&-3\\
0&-1&-1&-2\\
0&0&-2&1\\
0&0&0&-1
\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Because $L$ has unit diagonal and $U$ is <upper triangular matrix>[upper triangular],
$$
\boxed{\det A=\det L\det U=3(-1)(-2)(-1)=-6}.
$$

Solving the two triangular systems gives
$$
Ly=b
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
y=(3,-3,-1,-1)^T,
$$
and
$$
Ux=y
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\boxed{x=(3,0,1,1)^T}.
$$

= 9H
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Markov Chains}

= a
{parent=9h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $R$ be the number of returns to $v$ after time $0$. Since $v$ is a <recurrent state>, the probability of another return after each visit is one. By the stated <geometric distribution> description, $\mathbb P_v(R\geq k)=1$ for every positive integer $k$. Thus $R=\infty$ almost surely and $\mathbb E_vR=\infty$.

On the other hand, writing each visit as an <indicator random variable> and applying the <monotone convergence theorem> to the partial sums gives
$$
1+\mathbb E_vR
=\mathbb E_v\left[\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\boldsymbol1_{\{X_n=v\}}\right]
=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\mathbb P_v(X_n=v)
=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}p_{vv}(n).
$$
The left side is infinite, proving the <recurrence criterion by return probabilities> in the required direction:
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}p_{vv}(n)=\infty}.
$$

= b
{parent=9h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

By <independent random variables>[independence] of the two <simple random walk>[simple random walks], <linearity of expectation>, and symmetry of their transition probabilities,
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E Z
&=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\mathbb P(X_n=Y_n)\\
&=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\sum_{x\in\mathbb Z}p_{0x}(n)^2\\
&=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\sum_{x\in\mathbb Z}p_{0x}(n)p_{x0}(n).
\end{aligned}
$$
The <Chapman-Kolmogorov equation> identifies the inner sum as $p_{00}(2n)$, so
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E Z=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}p_{00}(2n)}.
$$
A <simple random walk on the integer line> can return to $0$ only at an <even number>[even] time. Since it is recurrent at $0$, part (a) gives
$$
\sum_{m=0}^{\infty}p_{00}(m)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}p_{00}(2n)=\infty.
$$
Therefore $\boxed{\mathbb E Z=\infty}$.

= 10F
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Linear Algebra}

= Solution
{parent=10F}

For $u\in U$, define $\phi(u)\in U^*$ by $\phi(u)(u')=\langle u,u'\rangle$. If $\phi(u)=0$, then in particular
$$
0=\phi(u)(u)=\langle u,u\rangle=\lVert u\rVert^2,
$$
so $u=0$. Thus $\phi$ is <injective>. Since a finite-dimensional vector space and its <dual space> have the same <dimension of a vector space>[dimension], $\phi$ is also <surjective function>[surjective] and hence an <isomorphism>. This is the finite-dimensional real case of the <Riesz representation theorem>.

The <adjoint operator> of $\alpha:V\to W$ is the unique linear map $\alpha^*:W\to V$ satisfying
$$
\langle\alpha v,w\rangle_W=\langle v,\alpha^*w\rangle_V
$$
for every $v\in V$ and $w\in W$. In the stated <orthonormal basis>[orthonormal bases], if $v$ and $w$ denote coordinate columns, then
$$
\langle Av,w\rangle=(Av)^Tw=v^TA^Tw.
$$
Therefore the matrix of $\alpha^*$ is the <matrix transpose> $A^T$.

For $w\in W$,
$$
w\in\ker\alpha^*
\iff \langle v,\alpha^*w\rangle=0\text{ for every }v
\iff \langle\alpha v,w\rangle=0\text{ for every }v
\iff w\in(\operatorname{im}\alpha)^\perp.
$$
Hence $\ker\alpha^*=(\operatorname{im}\alpha)^\perp$. Taking orthogonal complements in the finite-dimensional space $W$ proves the <image-kernel orthogonality for an adjoint>
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{im}\alpha=(\ker\alpha^*)^\perp}.
$$

Put $r=\alpha(v_0)-w_0$. For any $h\in V$,
$$
\lVert\alpha(v_0+h)-w_0\rVert^2
=\lVert r\rVert^2+2\langle r,\alpha h\rangle+\lVert\alpha h\rVert^2.
$$
This is minimized at $h=0$ exactly when $r\perp\operatorname{im}\alpha$, equivalently when $\alpha^*r=0$. Thus the <normal equation for a linear inverse problem> is
$$
\boxed{\alpha^*\alpha(v_0)=\alpha^*(w_0)}.
$$

For the given <linear least-squares problem>,
$$
A^TA=\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\1&2\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
A^Tb=\begin{pmatrix}3\\5\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The normal equations $2x+y=3$ and $x+2y=5$ have the unique solution
$$
\boxed{x=\frac13,\qquad y=\frac73}.
$$

= 11G
